ng the feelings of  monotony and boredom descended upon me  with no adversaries to fight back  against them I didn't see the side  quests as the beacons of hope they were  meant to be see in rock 8 I completed a  good majority of the side quests they  were fun and written well and did a  great job of world-building but they  didn't feel like they included a lot of  key information that would drastically  improve or change my understanding of  the game they definitely helped to paint  a better picture of the universe I was  in and I did enjoy them but I didn't get  a feeling of weight and most of them and  that was totally ok they're side quests  after all I have the main quest to learn  key information so I don't need side  quests for that this meant that I didn't  give a huge amount of emphasis to the  importance of side quests and then in  route B when the main quests often  lacked new key information or at least  lacked what I could recognize as new key  information  I didn't know to prioritize the side  classes as a result I completed  drastically fewer side quests and was in  more of a rush to progress the main  quest and to get to a point where I  started getting new key information and  story progression this calls my route be  played through to suffer a lot I was  essentially deprived of a significant  amount of story information and world  context while having nothing to break up  the monotony inherent in route feat this  left me feeling underwhelmed once I  actually finished the entire game I was  somewhat upset that that was it I very  much think that my not so great  experience with the route be tainted my  overall appreciation of the game  especially when I first finished it  but then I saw some videos talking about  why people think automata is such a good  game and why it's so luck and through  this I was able to make a connection  that really let me understand and see a  Tomita and a new light I'll try to  explain this epiphany if I can call it  that