ctful side quests to the  required missions group pretty simple  and I don't think it would ruin the flow  or the design philosophy of the game too  much it could still maintain the  hands-off storytelling approach by  keeping lots of information inside  quests and small world details but it  would make sure that no player slips  through the cracks of the main focus the  narrative of the game at the end of the  day maybe this design was completely  intentional by Yoko taro  and if so I respect that but I think it  was the wrong design choice I guess I  feel that the hands-off approach was  applied just a little too strongly for a  narrative based game for most people it  didn't make a difference which means  even if this is a flaw it isn't too big  of one on the large scale but still  cleaning this mechanic up can make the  game that much better which at the end  of the day is what we would all want for  games to keep getting better and I would  like to reiterate that nee automata  definitely did achieve this it has  continued the tradition of raising the  bar for great video games and all those  involved in making it should be very  proud this video just showed my single  biggest gripe with it and with so many  people already heaping on praise for the  game I thought it made sense to offer up  some criticism let me know what you guys  think about automata and Robby and Yoko  Tardos design choices I'd like to thank  the youtuber Valkyrie Aurora for videos  on near which are the ones I mentioned  that helped me make the connection  between automata and the souls series  and see automata in a more sympathetic  light this video actually shows my views  in the most positive they've been in  since I finished the game if you're  interested in automata or any of the  rest of its series she has a lot of good  videos on it I may make some more  automata videos but we'll see how people  like this one and how much  more I have to say thank you for  watching and have the swaggiest of days

